    "question": {
      ":question/id": "e09298eb-6641-596c-85c6-e0a85c88274f",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following diagrams gives the slope field at the points indicated for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2-(3)xy?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(3,1)$,$(-1,3)$,$(0,-1)$.\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(3,1)}=2-3(3)(1)=-7, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1,3)}=2-3(-1)(3)=11, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,-1)}=2-3(0)(-1)=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following diagram.\n\n![](aeb3c0a2d6cd2eb8ef47aa1f24a83e61cb59b3b212ebfb1097d73bc68691d25d.png)",
