      ":question/id": "00086532-45a9-52dd-92d0-42fe8e91002c",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following diagrams gives the slope field at the points indicated for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=1x^{2}-(2)y?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(2,-1)$,$(0,1)$,$(2,2)$.\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(2,-1)}=1(2)^{2}-2(-1)=6, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,1)}=1(0)^{2}-2(1)=-2, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(2,2)}=1(2)^{2}-2(2)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following diagram.\n\n![](8f3cdc5d5f5f31d57c15b5182cdedee8b0201e1c6e852c76cd52321e706551d7.png)",
