      ":question/problem": "Which of the following diagrams gives the slope field at the points indicated for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=1\\frac{y^{2}}{x}?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(1,-1)$,$(-1,-3)$,$(2,-2)$.\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,-1)}=1\\frac{(-1)^{2}}{(1)}=1, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1,-3)}=1\\frac{(-3)^{2}}{(-1)}=-9, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(2,-2)}=1\\frac{(-2)^{2}}{(2)}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following diagram.\n\n![](d2080a1138a06d9cfe6d423ac9f9017d6ac9e66cbe6120782844ad9a5d46f291.png)",
